1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:08,735
In 1995 and 1996, sixty-six gray wolves were released into central Idaho and Yellowstone National Park,

2
00:00:08,735 --> 00:00:13,240
about seventy years after the last of Yellowstone’s wolves were killed.

3
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:17,990
Their descendants now number at least twenty-eight hundred, across six states.

4
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:21,620
Within twenty years a story had traveled around the world.

5
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:25,897
The wolves had frightened the elk, the willows and aspen had grown back,

6
00:00:25,897 --> 00:00:28,320
and even the rivers had changed their course.

7
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:30,440
It is a beautiful story.

8
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:31,520
Is it true?

9
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:38,030
To answer, we have to pull apart three things that are easy to blur.

10
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:40,040
What did anyone actually observe?

11
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:43,810
What was inferred from those observations, and under which assumptions?

12
00:00:44,210 --> 00:00:46,140
And what are we entitled to claim we know?

13
00:00:46,890 --> 00:00:50,991
Ecologists have argued over those answers for a quarter of a century,

14
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:54,260
counting elk, measuring aspen, and collaring predators.

15
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:58,110
The argument is one of the best lessons there is in how science works.

16
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:04,249
So this film follows predators, and what they do and do not do to the world around them,

17
00:01:04,249 --> 00:01:08,169
through the philosophy of science: what science is trying to do,

18
00:01:08,169 --> 00:01:12,272
how we can know anything about nature, induction and falsification,

19
00:01:12,272 --> 00:01:17,110
paradigms and models, causes and confounders, Bayesian belief, and replication.

20
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:23,010
And we will ask a harder question: what counts as science, and who gets to decide?

21
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:25,610
Two guides will help.

22
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:30,810
The badge in the corner shows whether an idea concerns observation, inference, or claim.

23
00:01:31,210 --> 00:01:35,050
The lexicon on the right collects the terms an examiner may ask you to define.

24
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:39,230
Chapter one.

25
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:43,070
The aims of science: what all this effort is for.

26
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:46,950
Begin with one herd.

27
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:48,740
Just before wolves returned,

28
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:53,060
the elk wintering on Yellowstone’s Northern Range numbered more than nineteen thousand.

29
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:57,800
By 2013 the count had fallen to 3,915.

30
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,820
Today it is roughly seven to nine thousand.

31
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:03,670
Numbers like these invite different aims.

32
00:02:04,070 --> 00:02:06,220
Explain: why did the herd decline?

33
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:09,410
Predict: will willows along the streams recover?

34
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:13,110
Intervene: would killing predators bring the elk back?

35
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:15,180
The aims can come apart.

36
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:21,038
Predators kill many young ungulates, yet across fifty-two predator-removal experiments,

37
00:02:21,038 --> 00:02:26,370
removal often raised juvenile survival but rarely raised adult survival or abundance.

38
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:30,410
A true causal fact does not guarantee the outcome of an intervention.

39
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:36,420
Description, explanation, prediction, understanding, and control are all aims of science.

40
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:38,507
Traditions rank them differently,

41
00:02:38,507 --> 00:02:42,800
and much confusion about what science is begins with confusion about what it is for.

42
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:49,198
In 1948, Carl Hempel and Paul Oppenheim proposed that to explain

43
00:02:49,198 --> 00:02:53,320
an event is to deduce it from general laws and initial conditions.

44
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,760
On their deductive-nomological model,

45
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:59,840
explanation and prediction are the same argument, made at different times.

46
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:01,610
The symmetry breaks.

47
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:05,788
A flagpole’s shadow and the sun’s angle let you deduce the pole’s height,

48
00:03:05,788 --> 00:03:07,600
but the shadow does not explain it.

49
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,786
A correlative distribution model can predict where a species lives without saying why,

50
00:03:12,786 --> 00:03:16,570
and a mechanistic model can explain a process yet predict it poorly.

51
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:22,150
Wesley Salmon answered that explanation shows the causes and mechanisms that produced an event.

52
00:03:22,550 --> 00:03:26,870
Philip Kitcher answered that it unifies many phenomena under a few patterns of argument.

53
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:29,928
Trophic cascade theory aspires to both:

54
00:03:29,928 --> 00:03:35,040
one mechanism linking otters and kelp, wolves and aspen, spiders and grass.

55
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,310
When a theory works, is it true?

56
00:03:39,710 --> 00:03:42,370
Scientific realists say yes, approximately.

57
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:45,299
Hilary Putnam argued that realism is the only view

58
00:03:45,299 --> 00:03:47,930
that does not make the success of science a miracle.

59
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,187
Larry Laudan replied with history: the ether, caloric,

60
00:03:52,187 --> 00:03:55,370
and phlogiston all succeeded, and all were false.

61
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:58,720
If success did not guarantee truth then, why now?

62
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:01,190
This is the pessimistic meta-induction.

63
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:03,960
Antirealists offer humbler aims.

64
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:06,730
Instrumentalists treat theories as tools.

65
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:12,470
Bas van Fraassen asks only that a theory be empirically adequate, true about what is observable.

66
00:04:12,870 --> 00:04:16,880
Structural realists keep the mathematical structure that survives theory change.

67
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:21,449
Ecology is full of things no one observes directly: niches,

68
00:04:21,449 --> 00:04:25,010
carrying capacity, predation risk, a landscape of fear.

69
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:27,850
Real features of the world, or useful summaries?

70
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:30,510
Your answer shapes what you think a study has shown.

71
00:04:32,910 --> 00:04:35,480
This film sorts every idea into three bands.

72
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:39,710
Observation: records made by senses and instruments.

73
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:44,130
Inference: reasoning from records through models and assumptions.

74
00:04:44,530 --> 00:04:49,750
Claim: a statement of knowledge, with its scope and confidence, offered for scrutiny.

75
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:53,550
The bands follow Stephen Toulmin’s model of an argument.

76
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:56,236
Data support a claim through a warrant,

77
00:04:56,236 --> 00:04:59,870
a general rule that licenses the step, which rests on backing.

78
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:05,400
A qualifier says how strongly the claim holds; a rebuttal says when it would not.

79
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:07,170
Try it on the herd.

80
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:11,020
Data: elk counts fell after 1995.

81
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:13,630
Warrant: predators can reduce prey.

82
00:05:14,030 --> 00:05:16,400
Claim: wolves caused the decline.

83
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,390
Qualifier: in part.

84
00:05:18,790 --> 00:05:22,555
Rebuttal: unless hunting and drought did most of the work, which,

85
00:05:22,555 --> 00:05:26,030
for the first decade, a demographic analysis found they did.

86
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:32,379
Each band adds assumptions, and each fails in its own way: observations can be biased,

87
00:05:32,379 --> 00:05:36,220
inferences confounded, and claims can outrun their warrant.

88
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:39,180
Chapter two.

89
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:43,430
Knowing nature: what knowledge is, and the traditions that seek it.

90
00:05:46,130 --> 00:05:50,280
Epistemology is the study of knowledge: what it is, and how we get it.

91
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:53,760
A classic answer says knowledge is justified true belief.

92
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:57,520
You believe it, it is true, and you have good reason to believe it.

93
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:01,900
In 1963, Edmund Gettier showed that is not enough.

94
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:07,530
A biologist glimpses a coyote, takes it for a wolf, and concludes a wolf is in the valley.

95
00:06:07,930 --> 00:06:09,990
One is, out of sight behind a ridge.

96
00:06:10,390 --> 00:06:14,390
The belief is justified and true, but it is luck, not knowledge.

97
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:16,830
Where does justification come from?

98
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:21,270
Empiricists answer experience; rationalists, reason.

99
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:24,795
Foundationalists rest knowledge on basic beliefs;

100
00:06:24,795 --> 00:06:27,920
coherentists, on how beliefs support one another.

101
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:32,790
And David Hume warned that no amount of past experience logically guarantees the future.

102
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:37,787
Most scientists today are fallibilists, after Charles Sanders Peirce:

103
00:06:37,787 --> 00:06:41,910
any belief might be wrong, so inquiry must stay open to correction.

104
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:46,730
Willard Quine went further and treated epistemology itself as part of science,

105
00:06:46,730 --> 00:06:48,770
the study of how real knowers learn.

106
00:06:51,170 --> 00:06:54,160
Research traditions differ in how they answer four questions.

107
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:56,800
Ontology: what exists to be known?

108
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:59,420
Epistemology: how can we know it?

109
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:02,690
Methodology: how do we go about finding out?

110
00:07:03,090 --> 00:07:06,830
Axiology: what do we value in research, and what do we owe?

111
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,749
Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln compared research paradigms by the first three,

112
00:07:11,749 --> 00:07:13,230
and later added the fourth.

113
00:07:13,630 --> 00:07:18,250
A warning about the word: paradigm here means a broad research tradition,

114
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:22,110
not quite Thomas Kuhn’s sense, which we meet in chapter five.

115
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:25,619
We will follow two broad traditions:

116
00:07:25,619 --> 00:07:30,600
positivism and its successor, postpositivism; and interpretivism.

117
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,530
Each is internally diverse, none is a single view, and neither belongs to a single discipline.

118
00:07:38,930 --> 00:07:41,754
Auguste Comte coined positivism in the 1830s:

119
00:07:41,754 --> 00:07:47,150
knowledge comes from observation and the laws that summarize it, not from metaphysics.

120
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:51,448
In the 1920s the Vienna Circle sharpened it into logical positivism,

121
00:07:51,448 --> 00:07:55,690
and A. J. Ayer carried its verification principle into English philosophy.

122
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:59,171
A statement is meaningful, the principle said,

123
00:07:59,171 --> 00:08:02,970
only if observation could verify it or it is true by definition.

124
00:08:03,370 --> 00:08:06,570
The principle failed by its own test: it is neither.

125
00:08:06,970 --> 00:08:09,197
And it would have ruled out much of physics,

126
00:08:09,197 --> 00:08:11,830
whose theoretical terms are never observed directly.

127
00:08:12,580 --> 00:08:15,870
What most ecologists practice today is postpositivism.

128
00:08:16,270 --> 00:08:22,364
A real world exists but is imperfectly known; every observation is fallible; so we use design,

129
00:08:22,364 --> 00:08:27,940
statistics, and replication to approach the truth, and hold conclusions provisionally.

130
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:30,960
Critical realism gives this stance its philosophy.

131
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:36,227
In the late nineteenth century, Wilhelm Dilthey argued

132
00:08:36,227 --> 00:08:38,670
that the human sciences need their own method.

133
00:08:39,070 --> 00:08:43,090
Nature we explain, from outside, by causes: Erklären.

134
00:08:43,490 --> 00:08:48,500
Human life we understand, from inside, through meanings: Verstehen.

135
00:08:49,250 --> 00:08:51,747
Max Weber built Verstehen into sociology,

136
00:08:51,747 --> 00:08:56,620
and Charles Taylor argued that the social sciences are irreducibly interpretive.

137
00:08:57,020 --> 00:09:00,830
Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann showed how much of social reality,

138
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:05,890
from property lines to protected-area boundaries, is constructed and sustained by people.

139
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,450
Wildlife science meets interpretivism wherever people are part of the system.

140
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:14,423
Whether a wolf is a symbol of wilderness, a threat to a ranch,

141
00:09:14,423 --> 00:09:17,650
or a rival for the hunter’s elk depends on who is asked.

142
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:22,620
Ecologists call the number of carnivores people will tolerate the social carrying capacity.

143
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:27,390
Look at the Northern Range elk through each lens.

144
00:09:27,790 --> 00:09:33,350
A postpositivist counts animals, estimates survival, and models the causes of decline.

145
00:09:33,750 --> 00:09:37,170
The question is what happened, and how much each factor contributed.

146
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,432
An interpretivist asks what the herd means to ranchers, hunters,

147
00:09:41,432 --> 00:09:45,054
outfitters, and visitors, and how those meanings shape management,

148
00:09:45,054 --> 00:09:49,280
from hunting quotas to tolerance of elk on irrigated fields outside the park.

149
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:54,260
One herd, two kinds of question; each lens shows something the other misses.

150
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:57,220
Chapter three.

151
00:09:57,620 --> 00:10:01,330
Inference: deduction, induction, and abduction.

152
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:09,800
Aldo Leopold supported killing predators in the 1910s and 1920s, then changed his mind.

153
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:14,072
On Arizona’s Kaibab Plateau, he wrote, deer had irrupted after wolves

154
00:10:14,072 --> 00:10:18,000
and pumas were removed, then stripped the range of browse and starved.

155
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:21,430
Rachel Carson retold the story in Silent Spring.

156
00:10:21,830 --> 00:10:25,504
It became a founding example for the green world hypothesis:

157
00:10:25,504 --> 00:10:29,300
the world is green because predators keep herbivores in check.

158
00:10:30,050 --> 00:10:33,332
In 1970, Graeme Caughley examined the Kaibab records

159
00:10:33,332 --> 00:10:36,110
and called them unreliable and inconsistent.

160
00:10:36,510 --> 00:10:40,263
He suggested the deer increase may have followed the removal of cattle,

161
00:10:40,263 --> 00:10:42,430
a rival explanation no one had ruled out.

162
00:10:42,830 --> 00:10:45,950
A general rule had been built on one poorly measured case.

163
00:10:48,350 --> 00:10:51,020
Deduction moves from premises to what must follow.

164
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:54,870
If all elk are ungulates, and this is an elk, it is an ungulate.

165
00:10:55,270 --> 00:10:59,240
A valid deduction cannot have true premises and a false conclusion;

166
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:01,610
a sound one is valid with true premises.

167
00:11:02,010 --> 00:11:04,920
But deduction never says more than its premises contain.

168
00:11:05,670 --> 00:11:12,120
Induction generalizes from cases: every swan seen so far is white, so all swans are white.

169
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:15,590
It is ampliative, adding content, and fallible.

170
00:11:15,990 --> 00:11:20,650
David Hume showed it cannot be justified without assuming the future will resemble the past.

171
00:11:21,050 --> 00:11:24,260
Europeans learned this in Australia, from black swans.

172
00:11:25,010 --> 00:11:27,400
Abduction, Charles Sanders Peirce’s term,

173
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:31,540
reasons from a surprising fact to the hypothesis that would explain it.

174
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:37,267
Peter Lipton calls this inference to the best explanation, and warns that the loveliest explanation,

175
00:11:37,267 --> 00:11:40,730
the one that would explain the most, is not always the likeliest.

176
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:44,295
The Kaibab story was an induction from one case,

177
00:11:44,295 --> 00:11:47,930
joined to an abduction whose loveliness hid an untested rival.

178
00:11:50,330 --> 00:11:51,840
Science runs the three together.

179
00:11:52,240 --> 00:11:55,270
The hypothetico-deductive method takes a hypothesis,

180
00:11:55,270 --> 00:11:58,650
deduces what should be observed if it is true, and checks.

181
00:11:59,050 --> 00:12:02,020
In Yellowstone, aspen grew taller after wolves returned.

182
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:04,580
The abduction: wolves did it.

183
00:12:05,330 --> 00:12:05,860
But how?

184
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:11,090
If fear kept elk away from risky places, browsing should drop most where wolves hunt.

185
00:12:11,490 --> 00:12:16,080
If wolves simply reduced elk numbers, recovery should track elk density everywhere.

186
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:20,370
Two mechanisms, two different predictions, testable in the field.

187
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:25,513
In 1890, Thomas Chamberlin urged scientists to hold multiple working hypotheses,

188
00:12:25,513 --> 00:12:27,710
so they would not fall in love with one.

189
00:12:28,110 --> 00:12:32,050
In 1964, John Platt called the method strong inference:

190
00:12:32,050 --> 00:12:37,350
devise rival hypotheses, design experiments that exclude some, and repeat.

191
00:12:37,750 --> 00:12:43,060
In 2015, Adam Ford and Jacob Goheen asked trophic-cascade research to do exactly that.

192
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:46,040
Chapter four.

193
00:12:46,440 --> 00:12:48,450
Falsification, and why it is not enough.

194
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:53,670
Karl Popper turned Hume’s problem around.

195
00:12:54,070 --> 00:12:57,068
No number of white swans proves that all swans are white,

196
00:12:57,068 --> 00:12:59,120
but one black swan proves they are not.

197
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:02,890
Verification and falsification are logically asymmetric.

198
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:08,730
So science, Popper argued, advances by bold conjectures that risk refutation.

199
00:13:09,130 --> 00:13:13,220
A theory that survives a severe test is corroborated, never proven.

200
00:13:13,620 --> 00:13:18,220
In 1919, Einstein’s theory predicted how far starlight would bend around the sun.

201
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:19,500
It could have failed.

202
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:20,590
It did not.

203
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:26,080
Falsifiability became Popper’s criterion for separating science from pseudoscience.

204
00:13:26,480 --> 00:13:30,480
A theory that could explain any outcome, whatever happened, explained nothing.

205
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:34,180
Here is a bold conjecture.

206
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:38,996
Wolves reduce elk numbers not only by killing them but by frightening them:

207
00:13:38,996 --> 00:13:41,940
vigilant, stressed elk eat less and conceive less.

208
00:13:42,340 --> 00:13:44,969
In 2007, Scott Creel and colleagues reported

209
00:13:44,969 --> 00:13:49,390
that elk progesterone fell as wolves became more numerous relative to elk.

210
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:52,415
Later studies measured pregnancy directly,

211
00:13:52,415 --> 00:13:55,610
from hunter-killed cows and from blood samples of live elk.

212
00:13:56,010 --> 00:13:59,440
Pregnancy rates were uniformly high, whatever the wolf risk.

213
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:03,590
And collared elk met wolves too rarely for fear to disrupt their foraging.

214
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:06,170
Is the fear hypothesis falsified?

215
00:14:06,570 --> 00:14:07,110
Not quite.

216
00:14:07,510 --> 00:14:10,914
The first test assumed that progesterone indexes pregnancy,

217
00:14:10,914 --> 00:14:13,510
and that the elk-to-wolf ratio measures risk.

218
00:14:13,910 --> 00:14:16,950
If either auxiliary was wrong, the hypothesis survives.

219
00:14:17,350 --> 00:14:22,190
Fear effects are well documented in behavior, but not yet shown to change elk demography.

220
00:14:24,590 --> 00:14:27,180
Pierre Duhem saw the problem in 1906.

221
00:14:27,580 --> 00:14:31,230
A prediction follows from a hypothesis plus auxiliary assumptions,

222
00:14:31,230 --> 00:14:33,830
so a failed prediction refutes only the bundle.

223
00:14:34,230 --> 00:14:36,210
Willard Quine generalized the point.

224
00:14:36,610 --> 00:14:38,890
Logic alone never says which part to blame.

225
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:42,020
Sometimes blaming an auxiliary is brilliant.

226
00:14:42,420 --> 00:14:44,937
When Uranus strayed from its predicted orbit,

227
00:14:44,937 --> 00:14:48,070
astronomers proposed an unseen planet and found Neptune.

228
00:14:48,470 --> 00:14:53,570
When Mercury strayed, the same move predicted a planet, Vulcan, that does not exist.

229
00:14:53,970 --> 00:14:55,600
The fault lay in Newton’s theory.

230
00:14:56,350 --> 00:14:58,550
Imre Lakatos made the choice principled.

231
00:14:58,950 --> 00:15:03,640
A research program has a hard core, protected by a belt of auxiliary hypotheses.

232
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:07,120
Adjusting the belt is progressive if it predicts new facts,

233
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:09,470
and degenerating if it only patches failures.

234
00:15:09,870 --> 00:15:12,250
All theories, he noted, are born refuted.

235
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,954
In Yellowstone, after a landscape test found no effect of predation risk on aspen browsing,

236
00:15:17,954 --> 00:15:21,601
proponents argued that elk sense risk where they first meet wolves,

237
00:15:21,601 --> 00:15:25,030
not where wolves kill them, nearly a kilometer away on average.

238
00:15:25,430 --> 00:15:28,840
Lakatos asks: did the adjustment predict anything new?

239
00:15:29,590 --> 00:15:30,400
Two cautions.

240
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:35,100
Probabilistic hypotheses cannot be strictly falsified by any single result.

241
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:39,588
And a null hypothesis significance test is not Popperian falsification:

242
00:15:39,588 --> 00:15:44,310
rejecting a null of no effect at all is not a risky test of a research hypothesis.

243
00:15:46,510 --> 00:15:47,300
Chapter five.

244
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:50,140
Paradigms, and how science changes its mind.

245
00:15:52,840 --> 00:15:58,543
In 1962, Thomas Kuhn described science not as a steady march of conjectures and refutations,

246
00:15:58,543 --> 00:16:02,510
but as long periods of normal science punctuated by revolutions.

247
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:08,369
In normal science a community shares a paradigm: a disciplinary matrix of theories,

248
00:16:08,369 --> 00:16:13,970
methods, and values, anchored by exemplars, model solutions that students learn to imitate.

249
00:16:14,370 --> 00:16:16,280
Research is puzzle-solving within it.

250
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:20,380
Anomalies are set aside, until they accumulate into a crisis.

251
00:16:21,130 --> 00:16:23,200
A revolution replaces the paradigm.

252
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:29,110
Kuhn compared it to a gestalt switch: the same figure seen first as a duck, then as a rabbit.

253
00:16:29,510 --> 00:16:33,970
Rival paradigms can be incommensurable, disagreeing about which problems matter

254
00:16:33,970 --> 00:16:38,260
and what would count as a solution, so no neutral test decides between them.

255
00:16:40,660 --> 00:16:44,909
For the first half of the twentieth century, killing predators was normal practice,

256
00:16:44,909 --> 00:16:47,930
carried out by government hunters across the American West.

257
00:16:48,330 --> 00:16:51,872
By 1994, wolves in the lower forty-eight states survived only

258
00:16:51,872 --> 00:16:55,530
around the northern Great Lakes and near Glacier National Park.

259
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:59,010
Leopold’s own reversal reads like a gestalt switch.

260
00:16:59,410 --> 00:17:03,889
In “Thinking Like a Mountain,” he describes watching a fierce green fire die in the eyes of

261
00:17:03,889 --> 00:17:08,270
a wolf he had shot, and realizing that the mountain saw the wolf differently than he did.

262
00:17:09,020 --> 00:17:14,505
A new program grew around top-down control, with its own exemplars: killer whales,

263
00:17:14,505 --> 00:17:21,327
sea otters, urchins, and kelp; ecological meltdown on predator-free islands in a Venezuelan reservoir;

264
00:17:21,327 --> 00:17:25,140
songbirds lost to mesopredators once coyotes disappeared.

265
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:27,450
Yellowstone became the textbook case.

266
00:17:28,200 --> 00:17:32,800
Normal science followed, extending the pattern to willow, cottonwood, and other parks.

267
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:35,890
So did anomalies, which we meet in the chapters ahead.

268
00:17:36,290 --> 00:17:40,950
Did ecology then undergo a revolution, or absorb its anomalies as context?

269
00:17:41,350 --> 00:17:43,130
Kuhn gives you the tools to decide.

270
00:17:45,530 --> 00:17:48,750
If there is no neutral test, is theory choice irrational?

271
00:17:49,150 --> 00:17:53,064
Kuhn said no. Scientists share values: accuracy,

272
00:17:53,064 --> 00:17:57,060
consistency, scope, simplicity, and fruitfulness.

273
00:17:57,460 --> 00:18:01,500
But they weigh them differently, so the values guide judgment without dictating it.

274
00:18:02,250 --> 00:18:03,700
Critics pushed both ways.

275
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:07,590
Lakatos thought programs could be ranked objectively by their progress.

276
00:18:07,990 --> 00:18:12,882
Paul Feyerabend argued that every rule of method has been broken by some great scientist,

277
00:18:12,882 --> 00:18:15,410
so the only universal rule is “anything goes.”

278
00:18:15,810 --> 00:18:19,180
Larry Laudan judged traditions by their success at solving problems.

279
00:18:19,930 --> 00:18:22,359
Ecology has its own candidate revolution,

280
00:18:22,359 --> 00:18:26,210
from the balance of nature to non-equilibrium and patch dynamics.

281
00:18:28,410 --> 00:18:29,260
Chapter six.

282
00:18:29,660 --> 00:18:31,700
Observation, and the theory it carries.

283
00:18:34,610 --> 00:18:36,780
Is there such a thing as a pure observation?

284
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:42,380
Norwood Russell Hanson asked us to imagine Tycho Brahe and Johannes Kepler on a hill at dawn.

285
00:18:42,780 --> 00:18:46,340
Tycho, who believed the earth stood still, sees the sun rise.

286
00:18:46,740 --> 00:18:48,680
Kepler sees the horizon turn away.

287
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:50,940
The same light, seen differently.

288
00:18:51,690 --> 00:18:55,753
Observation is theory-laden: what we notice, how we describe it,

289
00:18:55,753 --> 00:18:58,990
and what we record depend on the concepts we bring.

290
00:18:59,390 --> 00:19:02,110
James Bogen and James Woodward add a distinction.

291
00:19:02,510 --> 00:19:04,090
Data are the records we collect.

292
00:19:04,490 --> 00:19:08,240
Phenomena are the stable features of the world we infer from them,

293
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:10,570
and theories explain phenomena, not data.

294
00:19:12,970 --> 00:19:15,090
How do you see whether aspen are recovering?

295
00:19:15,490 --> 00:19:20,098
William Ripple and Robert Beschta measured the five tallest young aspen in each stand,

296
00:19:20,098 --> 00:19:23,260
reasoning that recovery would show first among the leaders.

297
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:27,442
Matthew Kauffman and colleagues measured every young aspen in random plots,

298
00:19:27,442 --> 00:19:29,660
and found no difference with predation risk.

299
00:19:30,410 --> 00:19:34,799
Revisiting the stands in 2012, Luke Painter and colleagues used both designs,

300
00:19:34,799 --> 00:19:41,070
random plots and the tallest stems, and drew their main range-wide conclusion largely from the random samples.

301
00:19:41,470 --> 00:19:46,270
Up to sixty-five percent of stands had at least one sapling taller than two hundred centimeters,

302
00:19:46,270 --> 00:19:48,570
the height at which aspen escape browsing elk.

303
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:52,310
Then Elaine Brice and colleagues compared the two designs.

304
00:19:52,710 --> 00:19:56,840
Both detected a cascade, but random sampling showed a much weaker signal.

305
00:19:57,240 --> 00:20:00,311
The tallest stems can show whether a cascade is happening;

306
00:20:00,311 --> 00:20:02,800
only a random sample can show how strong it is.

307
00:20:03,550 --> 00:20:09,310
Willow told a similar story: studies concentrated at well-watered sites overstated its recovery.

308
00:20:09,710 --> 00:20:11,180
Neither design was dishonest.

309
00:20:11,580 --> 00:20:15,120
Each embodied a different question, and the question shaped what was seen.

310
00:20:17,520 --> 00:20:19,350
Instruments are theory made physical.

311
00:20:19,750 --> 00:20:24,388
Early studies tracked elk with VHF radio collars, located a few times a month,

312
00:20:24,388 --> 00:20:27,480
and could only map predation risk as fixed in space.

313
00:20:27,880 --> 00:20:34,210
GPS collars on elk, wolves, and pumas, located many times a day, revealed something new.

314
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:38,628
Elk moved into open country at night and into forest by day,

315
00:20:38,628 --> 00:20:44,680
avoiding the peak hours of both predators: wolves hunting open terrain, pumas hunting rough forest.

316
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:46,858
This dynamic landscape of fear,

317
00:20:46,858 --> 00:20:50,700
invisible to the earlier instrument, left elk ample time to browse.

318
00:20:51,450 --> 00:20:57,122
Before risk could be observed at all, it had to be defined: the core or edge of a wolf territory,

319
00:20:57,122 --> 00:21:01,800
the presence of downed logs, the width of a viewshed, or the locations of kills.

320
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:05,400
Each operational definition carried a theory of what elk fear.

321
00:21:06,150 --> 00:21:07,670
Your own data are no different.

322
00:21:08,070 --> 00:21:11,601
A lidar canopy height model is an inference from laser returns,

323
00:21:11,601 --> 00:21:14,180
classification thresholds, and a ground model.

324
00:21:14,580 --> 00:21:18,099
An animal count is abundance multiplied by detection probability,

325
00:21:18,099 --> 00:21:21,130
and not detecting a species is not proof of its absence.

326
00:21:23,530 --> 00:21:25,930
Theory-ladenness does not mean anything goes.

327
00:21:26,330 --> 00:21:30,580
The aspen stems were measured; they either grew past browse height or did not.

328
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:35,107
Ian Hacking argued that when independent methods, built on different theories,

329
00:21:35,107 --> 00:21:38,440
converge on the same result, we have good reason to believe it.

330
00:21:39,190 --> 00:21:44,247
Interpretivists meet the same problem with reflexivity: declaring the researcher’s position,

331
00:21:44,247 --> 00:21:47,270
because it shapes what is noticed and what is recorded.

332
00:21:49,470 --> 00:21:50,310
Chapter seven.

333
00:21:50,710 --> 00:21:52,950
Models, and why none of them is reality.

334
00:21:55,670 --> 00:21:59,900
A map at a scale of one to one would be perfectly accurate and perfectly useless.

335
00:22:00,300 --> 00:22:02,810
Models are valuable because they leave things out.

336
00:22:03,210 --> 00:22:07,460
The statistician George Box put it bluntly: all models are wrong.

337
00:22:08,210 --> 00:22:13,494
Michael Weisberg distinguishes three kinds of idealization: simplifying for tractability,

338
00:22:13,494 --> 00:22:19,490
keeping only the factors that make a difference, and building several incompatible models on purpose.

339
00:22:19,890 --> 00:22:23,911
Mary Morgan and Margaret Morrison describe models as mediators,

340
00:22:23,911 --> 00:22:26,720
partly independent of both theory and world.

341
00:22:27,470 --> 00:22:30,529
Richard Levins, whom you met in the first film,

342
00:22:30,529 --> 00:22:35,540
argued that no model can maximize generality, realism, and precision at once.

343
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:40,350
His remedy was robustness analysis: if several different models agree,

344
00:22:40,350 --> 00:22:43,500
the result is probably not an artifact of any one.

345
00:22:43,900 --> 00:22:47,730
“Our truth,” he wrote, “is the intersection of independent lies.”

346
00:22:50,130 --> 00:22:53,510
Wolf, elk, aspen: three boxes and two arrows.

347
00:22:53,910 --> 00:22:57,690
The model is clear, memorable, and wrong in ways that matter.

348
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:05,523
The system it stands for has pumas, which killed a larger share of Northern Range elk than wolves

349
00:23:05,523 --> 00:23:12,461
did in most years from 1998 to 2004; grizzly and black bears, the main predators of elk calves;

350
00:23:12,461 --> 00:23:18,960
human hunters; a bison herd that grew fourfold; and beaver, drought, and incised streams.

351
00:23:19,710 --> 00:23:22,990
Models also travel beyond the conditions that built them.

352
00:23:23,390 --> 00:23:28,190
One analysis estimated a fifteen-hundred-percent increase in willow crown volume.

353
00:23:28,590 --> 00:23:30,764
Critics argue that the volume equation,

354
00:23:30,764 --> 00:23:34,890
calibrated on lightly browsed plants, was applied to heavily browsed ones.

355
00:23:35,290 --> 00:23:39,450
An estimate is only as good as the model linking it to the quantity you care about.

356
00:23:41,850 --> 00:23:43,450
Models also act in the world.

357
00:23:43,850 --> 00:23:48,017
Early wolf-removal programs assumed moose were caught in a predator pit,

358
00:23:48,017 --> 00:23:52,590
a low-density state held by predators, with a high-density state waiting above.

359
00:23:52,990 --> 00:23:57,600
Kill enough wolves, the model said, and moose would climb out to a new equilibrium.

360
00:23:58,350 --> 00:24:03,409
François Messier found instead that wolf predation tends to hold moose at a single equilibrium,

361
00:24:03,409 --> 00:24:07,403
and a National Research Council review found little support for alternative

362
00:24:07,403 --> 00:24:09,320
stable states in wolf–moose systems.

363
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:12,640
A model of two states had helped justify real killing.

364
00:24:13,390 --> 00:24:15,256
Not every model is mathematical:

365
00:24:15,256 --> 00:24:19,280
a range map in a field guide and a hunting regulation are models too.

366
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:24,450
And a species distribution model estimates the realized niche, never the fundamental one.

367
00:24:26,650 --> 00:24:27,350
Chapter eight.

368
00:24:27,750 --> 00:24:30,540
Causality: association is not explanation.

369
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:35,890
Two variables can move together for many reasons.

370
00:24:36,290 --> 00:24:41,976
One may cause the other; the other may cause the one; a third variable may cause both;

371
00:24:41,976 --> 00:24:46,340
or the association may be manufactured by how cases were selected.

372
00:24:46,740 --> 00:24:50,320
Confounding, reverse causation, and collider bias.

373
00:24:51,070 --> 00:24:52,080
Here is a real puzzle.

374
00:24:52,480 --> 00:24:57,412
Across western North America, adult female elk survived better where both pumas

375
00:24:57,412 --> 00:25:01,470
and wolves lived than where there was only one predator, or none.

376
00:25:01,870 --> 00:25:03,430
Do two predators protect elk?

377
00:25:04,180 --> 00:25:05,310
Almost certainly not.

378
00:25:05,710 --> 00:25:09,533
Managers expecting heavy predation had already cut hunting quotas there,

379
00:25:09,533 --> 00:25:11,710
so management was a common cause of both.

380
00:25:12,110 --> 00:25:14,374
Across the same forty-five populations,

381
00:25:14,374 --> 00:25:17,740
human harvest lowered survival far more than any predator.

382
00:25:18,490 --> 00:25:20,372
A related trap is Simpson’s paradox,

383
00:25:20,372 --> 00:25:24,710
where a trend that holds within every subgroup reverses once the groups are pooled.

384
00:25:25,110 --> 00:25:28,220
The data alone cannot tell you whether to pool or stratify.

385
00:25:28,620 --> 00:25:29,880
That takes a causal model.

386
00:25:32,280 --> 00:25:33,200
What is a cause?

387
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:35,730
David Hume answered regular succession.

388
00:25:36,130 --> 00:25:39,982
David Lewis answered with counterfactuals: c caused e if,

389
00:25:39,982 --> 00:25:43,090
had c not happened, e would not have happened.

390
00:25:43,490 --> 00:25:46,168
James Woodward answered with intervention:

391
00:25:46,168 --> 00:25:49,930
c causes e if changing c, and nothing else, would change e.

392
00:25:50,680 --> 00:25:53,050
Statisticians formalized the counterfactual.

393
00:25:53,450 --> 00:25:57,480
Each unit has two potential outcomes, one with the treatment and one without.

394
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:02,302
Paul Holland called the catch the fundamental problem of causal inference:

395
00:26:02,302 --> 00:26:05,110
for any one unit, you observe only one of them.

396
00:26:05,860 --> 00:26:08,640
Wildlife biologists know this problem by another name.

397
00:26:09,040 --> 00:26:13,187
Mortality is additive if a predator’s kills add to deaths from other causes,

398
00:26:13,187 --> 00:26:16,570
and compensatory if the animals killed would have died anyway.

399
00:26:16,970 --> 00:26:20,300
That is a counterfactual claim about animals that are already dead.

400
00:26:21,050 --> 00:26:22,610
Evidence can still bear on it.

401
00:26:23,010 --> 00:26:27,904
In Yellowstone, wolf-killed cow elk were most often about sixteen years old;

402
00:26:27,904 --> 00:26:29,900
hunter-killed cows, about four.

403
00:26:30,300 --> 00:26:32,785
Old elk have little time left to live or breed,

404
00:26:32,785 --> 00:26:37,120
so wolf kills are more likely compensatory, and hunter kills more likely additive.

405
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:44,005
Judea Pearl gave causal reasoning a picture: the directed acyclic graph,

406
00:26:44,005 --> 00:26:47,120
in which arrows show which variables affect which.

407
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:51,710
Wolves, pumas, bears, hunters, and drought point at elk.

408
00:26:52,110 --> 00:26:55,460
Elk, bison, and water point at aspen and willow.

409
00:26:55,860 --> 00:26:57,150
Beaver points at water.

410
00:26:57,900 --> 00:26:59,750
The graph tells you what to adjust for.

411
00:27:00,150 --> 00:27:03,932
To estimate the effect of wolves on elk, block every back-door path,

412
00:27:03,932 --> 00:27:07,880
every route by which a common cause, such as management, links the two.

413
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:14,220
Pearl ranks causal questions on a ladder: association, intervention, and counterfactuals.

414
00:27:14,970 --> 00:27:18,760
Adam Ford and Jacob Goheen applied this logic to trophic cascades.

415
00:27:19,160 --> 00:27:23,665
Three links must be shown: predators suppress herbivores;

416
00:27:23,665 --> 00:27:28,170
herbivores suppress plants; and so predators help plants.

417
00:27:28,570 --> 00:27:32,650
In Yellowstone, exclosures establish the second link.

418
00:27:33,050 --> 00:27:36,480
The first is the least certain, and the third is mixed.

419
00:27:38,880 --> 00:27:41,332
Ronald Fisher’s answer was randomization:

420
00:27:41,332 --> 00:27:45,400
assign treatments by chance, and confounders balance out on average.

421
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:50,050
Crossing two treatments in a factorial design also reveals whether they interact.

422
00:27:50,800 --> 00:27:55,580
On the Northern Range, researchers crossed simulated beaver dams with elk exclosures.

423
00:27:55,980 --> 00:27:57,830
Either one alone sped willow growth.

424
00:27:58,230 --> 00:28:01,490
Together, they let willows pass browse height in about five years.

425
00:28:01,890 --> 00:28:04,600
Untreated controls never did, even after twenty.

426
00:28:05,350 --> 00:28:08,320
Paired experiments can separate top-down from bottom-up.

427
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:12,313
In Idaho, removing coyotes and pumas raised mule deer fawn

428
00:28:12,313 --> 00:28:15,720
survival slightly but did not change population growth.

429
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:20,760
In Colorado, feeding deer in winter raised population growth by thirteen percent a year.

430
00:28:21,510 --> 00:28:22,630
Weaker designs leak.

431
00:28:23,030 --> 00:28:25,768
Many predator-removal programs had no controls

432
00:28:25,768 --> 00:28:29,340
and relaxed hunting at the same time, confounding by design.

433
00:28:29,740 --> 00:28:32,287
And nature sometimes runs the experiment:

434
00:28:32,287 --> 00:28:38,190
when canine parvovirus collapsed Isle Royale’s wolves, moose grew faster and to higher numbers.

435
00:28:38,940 --> 00:28:42,733
When experiments are impossible, before-after-control-impact designs

436
00:28:42,733 --> 00:28:46,750
and Austin Bradford Hill’s viewpoints help weigh observational evidence.

437
00:28:47,150 --> 00:28:50,119
Interpretive social science adds another kind of cause:

438
00:28:50,119 --> 00:28:54,060
the reasons that move people, such as a rancher’s choice to tolerate elk.

439
00:28:56,260 --> 00:28:56,980
Chapter nine.

440
00:28:57,380 --> 00:29:00,800
Underdetermination: when evidence fits more than one theory.

441
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:07,020
Any finite set of points can be threaded by infinitely many curves.

442
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:10,597
Underdetermination is the general form of that fact:

443
00:29:10,597 --> 00:29:13,530
evidence alone may not fix which theory is true.

444
00:29:13,930 --> 00:29:19,070
Duhem and Quine’s holism makes it worse, because auxiliary assumptions can always be adjusted.

445
00:29:19,820 --> 00:29:25,262
Philosophers distinguish transient underdetermination, which better data or designs can resolve,

446
00:29:25,262 --> 00:29:30,420
from strong underdetermination, in which rival theories are empirically equivalent forever.

447
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:35,130
Most of what ecologists face is the transient kind, though it can last a long time.

448
00:29:35,880 --> 00:29:40,430
Hydrologists call it equifinality: many parameter sets fit the same record.

449
00:29:40,830 --> 00:29:43,500
Ecologists know it as the pattern–process problem.

450
00:29:43,900 --> 00:29:45,241
As the first film showed,

451
00:29:45,241 --> 00:29:49,370
neutral and niche models can produce the same species-abundance distribution.

452
00:29:51,770 --> 00:29:59,725
The Northern Range elk decline fits many stories: wolves; hunters taking females that left the park;

453
00:29:59,725 --> 00:30:06,010
drought; pumas; grizzly and black bears; competition with a growing bison herd.

454
00:30:06,410 --> 00:30:10,610
A demographic analysis attributed the first decade mainly to hunting and drought.

455
00:30:11,010 --> 00:30:14,110
No comparable analysis has apportioned the decline since.

456
00:30:14,860 --> 00:30:16,240
Some ties have been broken.

457
00:30:16,640 --> 00:30:21,760
Since 2005, the share of the herd wintering outside the park rose from twenty to eighty percent.

458
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:25,535
It looked like elk fleeing wolves, but it was demographic:

459
00:30:25,535 --> 00:30:29,550
elk outside the park survived better, rather than individuals moving.

460
00:30:30,300 --> 00:30:33,570
And a design built to separate mechanisms did separate them.

461
00:30:33,970 --> 00:30:38,881
Linking aspen recruitment to ten measures of predation risk and to changes in elk density,

462
00:30:38,881 --> 00:30:44,120
Brice and colleagues found risk effects eight to twenty-eight times weaker than density effects.

463
00:30:44,870 --> 00:30:46,600
For willow, the tie persists.

464
00:30:46,1000 --> 00:30:50,090
From the same data, one group sees a stalled system,

465
00:30:50,090 --> 00:30:55,320
possibly an alternative stable state held by bison, incised streams, and missing beaver.

466
00:30:55,720 --> 00:30:59,750
Another sees willow height doubled in twenty years: a slow recovery.

467
00:31:02,150 --> 00:31:04,740
How do scientists choose when evidence does not?

468
00:31:05,140 --> 00:31:09,768
With theoretical virtues, such as simplicity, scope, and coherence with other knowledge,

469
00:31:09,768 --> 00:31:12,660
though choosing among the virtues is itself a judgment.

470
00:31:13,410 --> 00:31:17,330
Better, design discriminating tests, as Chamberlin and Platt urged.

471
00:31:17,730 --> 00:31:19,300
Or weigh many models at once.

472
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:24,933
Kenneth Burnham and David Anderson’s multimodel inference ranks candidate models by information

473
00:31:24,933 --> 00:31:29,560
criteria such as AIC, and averages across them rather than crowning a single winner.

474
00:31:30,310 --> 00:31:32,430
Scale can also dissolve a contradiction.

475
00:31:32,830 --> 00:31:36,177
Large carnivores feed smaller carnivores through carrion nearby,

476
00:31:36,177 --> 00:31:37,850
and kill them across landscapes.

477
00:31:38,250 --> 00:31:42,296
Studies covering less than a thousand square kilometers tend

478
00:31:42,296 --> 00:31:46,410
to find positive associations; larger studies, negative ones.

479
00:31:46,810 --> 00:31:48,480
Both are right, at their own scale.

480
00:31:50,680 --> 00:31:51,440
Chapter ten.

481
00:31:51,840 --> 00:31:55,020
Bayesian epistemology: how evidence should change belief.

482
00:31:57,720 --> 00:32:00,920
Bayesian epistemology treats belief as a matter of degree.

483
00:32:01,320 --> 00:32:05,810
A credence is a probability, from zero to one, that a hypothesis is true.

484
00:32:06,210 --> 00:32:10,915
Frank Ramsey argued that credences should obey the laws of probability: if they do not,

485
00:32:10,915 --> 00:32:15,620
a clever bookie could sell you a set of bets that loses whatever happens, a Dutch book.

486
00:32:16,370 --> 00:32:19,240
Bayes’ theorem says how evidence should change a credence.

487
00:32:19,640 --> 00:32:24,645
The posterior probability of a hypothesis, given the evidence, is proportional to its prior

488
00:32:24,645 --> 00:32:29,980
probability times the likelihood: how probable the evidence would be if the hypothesis were true.

489
00:32:30,730 --> 00:32:33,420
Updating this way is called conditionalization.

490
00:32:33,820 --> 00:32:37,296
Evidence confirms a hypothesis when it raises its probability,

491
00:32:37,296 --> 00:32:39,370
and disconfirms it when it lowers it.

492
00:32:39,770 --> 00:32:42,080
On this view, confirmation comes in degrees.

493
00:32:44,480 --> 00:32:47,800
How strongly does evidence favor one hypothesis over another?

494
00:32:48,200 --> 00:32:49,867
The likelihood ratio answers:

495
00:32:49,867 --> 00:32:53,890
how much more probable the evidence is under one than under the other.

496
00:32:54,290 --> 00:32:57,330
It is the factor by which the evidence should multiply your odds.

497
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:00,900
Ignore the prior and you commit the base-rate fallacy.

498
00:33:01,300 --> 00:33:05,134
Suppose a camera-trap classifier labels ninety-five percent of wolf

499
00:33:05,134 --> 00:33:09,141
photos correctly and mislabels one percent of coyote photos as wolves,

500
00:33:09,141 --> 00:33:12,460
in a region where one canid photo in a thousand is a wolf.

501
00:33:12,860 --> 00:33:16,010
About nine in ten photos flagged as wolves are coyotes.

502
00:33:16,760 --> 00:33:21,309
Richard Royall separated three questions: what does the evidence say;

503
00:33:21,309 --> 00:33:24,210
what should I believe; and what should I do?

504
00:33:24,610 --> 00:33:26,030
They map onto our bands.

505
00:33:26,430 --> 00:33:30,385
The likelihood ratio answers the first, the posterior the second,

506
00:33:30,385 --> 00:33:32,940
and a decision, with its costs, the third.

507
00:33:35,340 --> 00:33:37,950
Read the Yellowstone debate as a sequence of updates.

508
00:33:38,350 --> 00:33:41,906
The prior came from Isle Royale, where tree rings tracked the rise

509
00:33:41,906 --> 00:33:44,870
and fall of wolves and moose, and from Leopold’s story.

510
00:33:45,270 --> 00:33:49,590
Early aspen studies raised belief in a strong cascade, driven by fear.

511
00:33:50,340 --> 00:33:55,706
Then a landscape test found no fear effect, random sampling found a weaker signal,

512
00:33:55,706 --> 00:33:58,520
density outweighed risk, and willow lagged.

513
00:33:58,920 --> 00:34:03,981
Belief in some cascade on aspen survived; belief that fear drives it fell;

514
00:34:03,981 --> 00:34:06,990
uncertainty about its strength remains wide.

515
00:34:07,740 --> 00:34:10,590
Bayesian reading also guards against a common error.

516
00:34:10,990 --> 00:34:16,720
Across sixteen pairs of large carnivores and mesocarnivores,

517
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:20,922
the average abundance correlation was −0.18,

518
00:34:20,922 --> 00:34:29,230
with a 95 percent confidence interval from −0.41 to 0.05: not significant, at p = 0.11.

519
00:34:29,980 --> 00:34:33,100
Not significant is not the same as no effect.

520
00:34:33,500 --> 00:34:36,585
Read with a flat prior, that interval puts the probability

521
00:34:36,585 --> 00:34:39,830
that the average effect is negative near ninety-four percent.

522
00:34:40,230 --> 00:34:43,960
The evidence leans toward suppression, but the data are mostly correlative.

523
00:34:46,360 --> 00:34:47,600
Where do priors come from?

524
00:34:47,1000 --> 00:34:49,430
Critics call them subjective.

525
00:34:49,830 --> 00:34:53,977
Bayesians reply that with enough shared evidence, different priors converge,

526
00:34:53,977 --> 00:34:59,870
because the data wash them out, and that writing priors down exposes assumptions other methods leave hidden.

527
00:35:00,620 --> 00:35:02,450
Deborah Mayo offers the main rival.

528
00:35:02,850 --> 00:35:07,720
On her error-statistical view, evidence supports a claim when the claim has passed a severe test,

529
00:35:07,720 --> 00:35:10,280
one it would probably have failed if it were false.

530
00:35:10,680 --> 00:35:14,450
The question is not how much to believe, but how well the claim has been probed.

531
00:35:16,650 --> 00:35:17,570
Chapter eleven.

532
00:35:17,970 --> 00:35:21,680
Replication: would it happen again, and elsewhere?

533
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:26,670
When a finding holds up, what exactly held?

534
00:35:27,070 --> 00:35:32,865
The National Academies distinguish reproducibility, where the same data and code give the same result,

535
00:35:32,865 --> 00:35:37,410
from replicability, where a new study with new data reaches a consistent result.

536
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:39,230
Two more words help.

537
00:35:39,630 --> 00:35:44,780
Generalizability asks whether a result extends to other places, times, and populations.

538
00:35:45,180 --> 00:35:48,840
Robustness asks whether it survives reasonable changes of analysis.

539
00:35:49,240 --> 00:35:51,594
When twenty-nine teams analyzed one data set,

540
00:35:51,594 --> 00:35:55,100
their estimates ranged from slightly negative to strongly positive.

541
00:35:57,500 --> 00:36:02,834
In 2005, John Ioannidis argued that most published research findings are false,

542
00:36:02,834 --> 00:36:06,750
given small samples, small effects, and flexible analyses.

543
00:36:07,150 --> 00:36:11,350
In 2015, a large collaboration repeated one hundred psychology studies.

544
00:36:11,750 --> 00:36:15,133
Ninety-seven percent of the originals had significant results;

545
00:36:15,133 --> 00:36:17,480
thirty-six percent of the replications did.

546
00:36:18,230 --> 00:36:24,159
The causes are familiar: low statistical power; publication bias toward positive results;

547
00:36:24,159 --> 00:36:28,222
p-hacking, trying analyses until one is significant; HARKing,

548
00:36:28,222 --> 00:36:32,819
presenting a hypothesis formed after the results as if it came first;

549
00:36:32,819 --> 00:36:39,480
and the garden of forking paths, where each reasonable choice raises the chance of a false positive.

550
00:36:40,230 --> 00:36:41,750
Ecology is not exempt.

551
00:36:42,150 --> 00:36:45,650
Many ecologists surveyed admit to questionable research practices.

552
00:36:46,050 --> 00:36:50,880
And Stuart Hurlbert showed in 1984 that many field experiments were pseudoreplicated,

553
00:36:50,880 --> 00:36:54,800
treating repeated measurements of one unit as independent replicates.

554
00:36:57,200 --> 00:37:02,538
In the Madison Headwaters of central Yellowstone, wolves met a non-migratory herd of four to

555
00:37:02,538 --> 00:37:08,050
six hundred elk, wintering on patches kept bare by thermal features and hemmed in by deep snow.

556
00:37:08,450 --> 00:37:13,390
Cow survival fell by five to fifteen percent, and elk numbers fell five- to tenfold.

557
00:37:14,140 --> 00:37:17,119
Less than thirty kilometers away, on the Northern Range,

558
00:37:17,119 --> 00:37:19,620
the effect of the same wolves is still debated.

559
00:37:20,020 --> 00:37:23,520
And on two Alaskan islands, wolves nearly eliminated the deer.

560
00:37:23,920 --> 00:37:25,870
Same predator, different worlds.

561
00:37:26,620 --> 00:37:29,360
Replication across many sites sharpens the pattern.

562
00:37:29,760 --> 00:37:35,220
Across eleven elk populations, only grizzly predation added to newborn calf mortality.

563
00:37:35,620 --> 00:37:38,840
Across forty-five, human harvest outweighed every predator.

564
00:37:39,240 --> 00:37:43,690
Across fifty-two removal experiments, killing predators rarely raised abundance.

565
00:37:44,440 --> 00:37:46,650
Generalizing also depends on where we look.

566
00:37:47,050 --> 00:37:51,345
About seventy percent of studies of carnivore interactions are in protected areas,

567
00:37:51,345 --> 00:37:55,536
yet only fifteen percent of large-carnivore range in North America is protected,

568
00:37:55,536 --> 00:37:58,260
and four percent is strictly protected from hunting.

569
00:37:59,010 --> 00:38:01,500
The review’s answer is a set of scope conditions.

570
00:38:01,900 --> 00:38:05,660
Predators most often limit their prey where prey are spatially confined,

571
00:38:05,660 --> 00:38:08,950
where they share a predator with a more resilient prey species,

572
00:38:08,950 --> 00:38:12,240
or where several predators specialize on different life stages.

573
00:38:14,640 --> 00:38:18,881
Remedies are spreading: preregistration and registered reports,

574
00:38:18,881 --> 00:38:22,852
which fix hypotheses and analyses before the data are seen;

575
00:38:22,852 --> 00:38:28,170
open data and code; and coordinated experiments run the same way at many sites.

576
00:38:28,570 --> 00:38:32,550
For predictive models, transferability must be tested, not assumed.

577
00:38:33,300 --> 00:38:35,530
Interpretive research has its own standards.

578
00:38:35,930 --> 00:38:41,009
Instead of validity and reliability, it asks for trustworthiness:

579
00:38:41,009 --> 00:38:46,010
credibility, transferability, dependability, and confirmability.

580
00:38:48,210 --> 00:38:48,900
Chapter twelve.

581
00:38:49,300 --> 00:38:51,400
Values, objectivity, and judgment.

582
00:38:54,410 --> 00:38:55,990
Should science be value-free?

583
00:38:56,390 --> 00:38:59,898
The traditional ideal says values may guide which questions we ask

584
00:38:59,898 --> 00:39:02,980
and how we use the answers, but not how we judge evidence.

585
00:39:03,380 --> 00:39:08,030
Hans Reichenbach separated the context of discovery from the context of justification.

586
00:39:08,780 --> 00:39:11,260
In 1953, Richard Rudner objected.

587
00:39:11,660 --> 00:39:16,741
Evidence never makes a hypothesis certain, so accepting one means judging the evidence strong enough,

588
00:39:16,741 --> 00:39:19,760
and how strong is enough depends on the cost of being wrong.

589
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:23,060
Heather Douglas calls this the argument from inductive risk.

590
00:39:23,810 --> 00:39:27,580
Epistemic values, such as accuracy and simplicity, bear on truth.

591
00:39:27,980 --> 00:39:30,965
Non-epistemic values, such as safety, livelihoods,

592
00:39:30,965 --> 00:39:34,010
or the survival of a species, bear on consequences.

593
00:39:34,410 --> 00:39:38,489
Douglas argues that the second kind may legitimately set evidential thresholds,

594
00:39:38,489 --> 00:39:40,400
but must never stand in for evidence.

595
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:47,762
Mountain caribou declined by fifty-one percent between 1991 and 2023,

596
00:39:47,762 --> 00:39:52,940
and fifteen of forty-one subpopulations are now functionally extirpated.

597
00:39:53,340 --> 00:39:58,109
Logging opened forests to moose and deer, which supported more wolves,

598
00:39:58,109 --> 00:40:01,380
which killed more caribou: apparent competition.

599
00:40:02,130 --> 00:40:04,700
Recovery actions have included killing wolves.

600
00:40:05,100 --> 00:40:10,150
Conclude that wolves are not limiting caribou when they are, and a population disappears.

601
00:40:10,550 --> 00:40:14,220
Conclude that they are when they are not, and wolves die for nothing.

602
00:40:14,620 --> 00:40:17,880
Which error is worse is not a question the data can answer alone.

603
00:40:18,630 --> 00:40:21,090
Values also shape which impacts get counted.

604
00:40:21,490 --> 00:40:26,118
Where wolves recolonized Wisconsin, deer–vehicle collisions fell by about a quarter,

605
00:40:26,118 --> 00:40:29,920
with benefits estimated at sixty-three times the losses of livestock.

606
00:40:30,320 --> 00:40:33,509
Puma recovery in the eastern United States was projected to

607
00:40:33,509 --> 00:40:36,860
prevent one hundred fifty-five human deaths over thirty years.

608
00:40:39,260 --> 00:40:40,500
What counts as recovery?

609
00:40:40,900 --> 00:40:44,322
Much of the disagreement over Yellowstone’s willow and aspen,

610
00:40:44,322 --> 00:40:49,090
Wilmers and colleagues conclude, stems from definitions and from uncertain baselines.

611
00:40:49,490 --> 00:40:53,762
Should recovery be measured against the landscape before European colonization,

612
00:40:53,762 --> 00:40:58,250
already shaped by Indigenous peoples, or against a landscape without people at all?

613
00:40:58,1000 --> 00:41:02,752
Michael Soulé called conservation biology a crisis discipline:

614
00:41:02,752 --> 00:41:09,410
it must act before the evidence is complete, and its goals, such as keeping species and processes, are values.

615
00:41:09,810 --> 00:41:12,198
Deciding how many carnivores a landscape should

616
00:41:12,198 --> 00:41:14,790
hold is an ecological question and a political one.

617
00:41:17,190 --> 00:41:20,970
If individual scientists carry values, where does objectivity come from?

618
00:41:21,370 --> 00:41:25,705
Robert Merton described the norms of science: communalism,

619
00:41:25,705 --> 00:41:30,040
universalism, disinterestedness, and organized skepticism.

620
00:41:30,440 --> 00:41:33,266
Helen Longino located objectivity in communities

621
00:41:33,266 --> 00:41:36,210
that expose claims to criticism and respond to it.

622
00:41:36,960 --> 00:41:39,496
Sandra Harding argued for strong objectivity:

623
00:41:39,496 --> 00:41:45,640
starting research from the standpoints of marginalized groups reveals assumptions a dominant view cannot see.

624
00:41:46,040 --> 00:41:49,902
Heather Douglas notes that objectivity is not one thing but several,

625
00:41:49,902 --> 00:41:52,230
from fair procedures to reliable results.

626
00:41:52,980 --> 00:41:55,670
The Yellowstone debate shows criticism at work.

627
00:41:56,070 --> 00:42:00,490
Comments and replies sharpened sampling designs, separated fear from density,

628
00:42:00,490 --> 00:42:03,820
and produced shared standards for demonstrating a cascade.

629
00:42:04,570 --> 00:42:06,290
The two traditions differ here too.

630
00:42:06,690 --> 00:42:10,910
Postpositivism aims at value neutrality while studying how values intrude.

631
00:42:11,310 --> 00:42:14,308
Interpretivism makes values explicit through reflexivity,

632
00:42:14,308 --> 00:42:17,570
treating the researcher’s commitments as part of the evidence.

633
00:42:19,770 --> 00:42:20,800
Chapter thirteen.

634
00:42:21,200 --> 00:42:24,630
Demarcation: what counts as science, and who decides.

635
00:42:27,330 --> 00:42:28,620
What counts as science?

636
00:42:29,020 --> 00:42:32,670
The demarcation problem sounds academic until a court must decide.

637
00:42:33,070 --> 00:42:36,798
In 1982, a federal judge struck down an Arkansas law requiring

638
00:42:36,798 --> 00:42:41,850
that creation science be taught alongside evolution, ruling that it was not science.

639
00:42:42,250 --> 00:42:45,880
In 2005, another court ruled the same of intelligent design.

640
00:42:46,630 --> 00:42:53,060
In 1993, the Supreme Court’s Daubert decision asked judges to screen expert testimony by testability,

641
00:42:53,060 --> 00:42:56,880
peer review, error rates, standards, and general acceptance.

642
00:42:57,280 --> 00:43:00,877
Funders, journals, and environmental assessments draw similar lines,

643
00:43:00,877 --> 00:43:03,840
and those lines decide whose knowledge shapes decisions.

644
00:43:06,240 --> 00:43:08,720
Each philosophy we have met offered a criterion.

645
00:43:09,120 --> 00:43:11,890
The logical positivists: verifiability.

646
00:43:12,290 --> 00:43:14,320
Popper: falsifiability.

647
00:43:14,720 --> 00:43:17,450
Kuhn: a tradition of puzzle-solving.

648
00:43:17,850 --> 00:43:21,040
Lakatos: a progressive research program.

649
00:43:21,440 --> 00:43:25,059
And Paul Thagard: a pseudoscience is a field that stagnates

650
00:43:25,059 --> 00:43:29,230
while its rivals progress, and whose practitioners ignore that fact.

651
00:43:29,980 --> 00:43:32,800
The courts leaned on methodological naturalism:

652
00:43:32,800 --> 00:43:38,080
science explains natural phenomena by natural causes, whatever one believes beyond them.

653
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:41,806
Merton and Longino point instead to institutions:

654
00:43:41,806 --> 00:43:45,200
open criticism, peer review, and shared standards.

655
00:43:45,950 --> 00:43:49,420
In 1983, Larry Laudan declared the problem dead.

656
00:43:49,820 --> 00:43:53,840
No set of necessary and sufficient conditions had survived counterexamples.

657
00:43:54,240 --> 00:43:59,720
Astrology makes testable claims and fails them; much good science is hard to falsify.

658
00:44:00,470 --> 00:44:02,320
Others revived it in looser form.

659
00:44:02,720 --> 00:44:06,103
Massimo Pigliucci treats science as a cluster concept,

660
00:44:06,103 --> 00:44:08,860
a family resemblance with no single essence.

661
00:44:09,260 --> 00:44:11,843
Paul Hoyningen-Huene argues that science differs

662
00:44:11,843 --> 00:44:14,480
from everyday knowledge by being more systematic.

663
00:44:14,880 --> 00:44:19,780
And the German word Wissenschaft names all systematic knowledge, from physics to history.

664
00:44:22,180 --> 00:44:26,940
Now apply the criteria to our two research traditions, with astrology as a contrast.

665
00:44:27,340 --> 00:44:31,510
Under verifiability, none fits cleanly; even physics strained it.

666
00:44:31,910 --> 00:44:38,319
Under falsifiability, postpositivist natural science fits by design, interpretive research fits in part,

667
00:44:38,319 --> 00:44:43,310
and astrology, awkwardly, fits too: its predictions can be tested, and they fail.

668
00:44:44,060 --> 00:44:47,520
Under institutional norms and Thagard’s test of progress,

669
00:44:47,520 --> 00:44:51,283
interpretive social research qualifies and astrology does not:

670
00:44:51,283 --> 00:44:58,020
no community criticizes and revises its claims, and it has stood still for centuries while its rivals advanced.

671
00:44:58,770 --> 00:45:02,386
Under cluster or systematicity accounts, or Wissenschaft,

672
00:45:02,386 --> 00:45:07,270
interpretive research qualifies easily; in German it has always been science.

673
00:45:07,670 --> 00:45:10,500
Whether English calls it science depends on the definition.

674
00:45:10,900 --> 00:45:12,990
The underlying practices do not change.

675
00:45:13,390 --> 00:45:14,470
Only the label does.

676
00:45:16,870 --> 00:45:19,890
Demarcation is usually aimed at fields outside science.

677
00:45:20,290 --> 00:45:22,060
The Kaibab story points inward.

678
00:45:22,460 --> 00:45:26,556
For decades, a weakly tested account of deer and predators lived in textbooks,

679
00:45:26,556 --> 00:45:31,230
retold by scientists and conservationists alike, until Caughley went back to the records.

680
00:45:31,980 --> 00:45:34,210
Nothing about its subject was unscientific.

681
00:45:34,610 --> 00:45:40,930
What failed was practice: one poorly measured case, an untested rival, and too little criticism.

682
00:45:41,330 --> 00:45:43,060
What corrected it was practice too.

683
00:45:43,810 --> 00:45:46,927
So demarcation concerns how claims are tested, criticized,

684
00:45:46,927 --> 00:45:49,560
and corrected, more than the prestige of a label.

685
00:45:49,960 --> 00:45:54,558
Under a narrow definition, postpositivist natural science is the central case

686
00:45:54,558 --> 00:45:58,320
and interpretive research is science in an institutional sense.

687
00:45:58,720 --> 00:46:00,700
Under a broad one, both qualify.

688
00:46:01,100 --> 00:46:02,520
The choice has consequences.

689
00:46:02,920 --> 00:46:03,830
Make it knowingly.

690
00:46:06,830 --> 00:46:07,800
Chapter fourteen.

691
00:46:08,200 --> 00:46:11,620
Synthesis: observe, infer, claim.

692
00:46:14,320 --> 00:46:18,790
Return to the claim that wolves restored Yellowstone, and audit it band by band.

693
00:46:19,190 --> 00:46:25,279
Observed: elk counts, from more than nineteen thousand to under four thousand,

694
00:46:25,279 --> 00:46:32,226
then back to seven to nine; aspen saplings escaping browse in many stands; willow taller,

695
00:46:32,226 --> 00:46:39,330
but mostly still below browse height; bison up fourfold; incised channels; few beaver dams.

696
00:46:40,080 --> 00:46:44,590
Inferred: browsing limits aspen and willow, as exclosures show.

697
00:46:44,990 --> 00:46:49,240
Several predators, and people, reduced elk, in shares not yet apportioned.

698
00:46:49,640 --> 00:46:52,310
Density effects outweighed fear effects on aspen.

699
00:46:52,710 --> 00:46:56,230
Each inference rests on sampling designs and assumptions we have examined.

700
00:46:56,980 --> 00:47:02,852
Claimable: large-carnivore recovery, together with hunting, drought, other predators,

701
00:47:02,852 --> 00:47:08,240
and bison, contributed to elk decline and to partial, local recovery of aspen.

702
00:47:08,640 --> 00:47:12,560
Willow recovery is constrained, perhaps in an alternative stable state.

703
00:47:12,960 --> 00:47:15,140
The strength of the cascade is still contested.

704
00:47:15,540 --> 00:47:18,380
Less lovely than the viral story, and better warranted.

705
00:47:19,130 --> 00:47:22,419
Across North America, Wilmers and colleagues conclude,

706
00:47:22,419 --> 00:47:28,570
humans are the dominant driver of ungulate dynamics, and predator limitation is real but conditional.

707
00:47:28,970 --> 00:47:32,150
Can predator restoration repair disrupted ecosystems?

708
00:47:32,550 --> 00:47:37,230
Sometimes, partly, and slowly, which is why preventing their loss matters most.

709
00:47:39,630 --> 00:47:43,590
Here is an audit you can take to any study, news story, or exam question.

710
00:47:43,990 --> 00:47:46,490
One: what was observed, and how?

711
00:47:46,890 --> 00:47:50,070
Two: what was inferred, under which assumptions?

712
00:47:50,470 --> 00:47:52,920
Three: which alternatives remain?

713
00:47:53,670 --> 00:47:57,130
Four: how strong is the evidence, and what would change your mind?

714
00:47:57,530 --> 00:48:01,430
Five: has it been replicated, and where does it generalize?

715
00:48:01,830 --> 00:48:05,190
Six: which values and stakes shape the claim?

716
00:48:05,590 --> 00:48:10,320
Seven: what would another tradition of inquiry ask that this one did not?

717
00:48:11,070 --> 00:48:12,790
No tradition owns these questions.

718
00:48:13,190 --> 00:48:16,594
Postpositivists and interpretivists answer them in their own ways,

719
00:48:16,594 --> 00:48:18,450
and each can hold the other to them.

720
00:48:20,850 --> 00:48:22,480
Before you go, rehearse.

721
00:48:22,880 --> 00:48:26,860
What separates induction from abduction, and where does strong inference fit?

722
00:48:27,260 --> 00:48:30,500
Why does the Duhem–Quine thesis complicate falsification?

723
00:48:30,900 --> 00:48:33,170
What did Kuhn mean by incommensurability?

724
00:48:33,920 --> 00:48:37,230
Why is compensatory mortality a counterfactual claim?

725
00:48:37,630 --> 00:48:39,680
What is the argument from inductive risk?

726
00:48:40,080 --> 00:48:44,800
And how does the answer to “what counts as science?” change with the criterion you choose?

727
00:48:46,550 --> 00:48:49,170
The ecological theater was a stage for evolution.

728
00:48:49,570 --> 00:48:51,330
This one has been a stage for knowing.

729
00:48:51,730 --> 00:48:56,200
Observe carefully, infer honestly, and claim no more than your warrant allows.
